  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>According to Bohr&#39;s postulates, if an electron absorbs a photon with energy equal to the difference between the energies of an excited state and the ground state, it will jump to a higher energy orbital.</p><p>Considering that the energy of the colliding electron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>09</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>, depending on how much energy is lost during the collision. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math> is the maximum&nbsp;excited state. Hence, there are spectral lines in a total of</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mmultiscripts><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn><none></none><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mn>3</mn></mmultiscripts></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>",
